          ":question/math-academy-id": "q-141200",
          ":question/problem": "Which of the following statements are true, given that $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix and  $det(A)=-1?$\n\nSelect all that apply.",
          ":question/worked-solution": "Since $A$ is a $6\\times 6$ matrix, the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}\\text{Col}(A) &={R}^{6} \\\\ \\text{dim(Col}(A)) &=6=\\text{rank}(A) \\\\ \\text{Null}(A) &=\\{0\\} \\\\ \\text{dim(Null}(A)) &=0=\\text{nullity}(A)\\end{aligned}\\right.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn. \n\n- Statement \u201c$A$ is invertible\u201d is true. Indeed,   a matrix $A$ is invertible if and only if $det(A)\\ne 0.$ Since here we have $det(A)=-1\\ne 0,$ our matrix $A$ must be  invertible.\n\n- Statement $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ is true. Indeed, by the invertible matrix theorem\n$A$ is invertible $\\,\u21d4\\,\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}.$\n\n- Statement $\\text{nullity}(A)=6$ is false. Since $A$ is invertible,  it must be $\\text{nullity}(A)=0.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \u201c$A$ is invertible\u201d; $\\text{Null}(A)=\\{0\\}$ only."
